===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ḱer- (grow)}}
From {{inh|en|enm|crue}}, from {{der|en|fro|creue|t=an increase, recruit, military reinforcement}}, the feminine past participle of {{m|fro|creistre|t=grow}}, from {{der|en|la|cresco|crescere|to arise, grow}}. {{doublet|en|creature|crescent|croissant|recruit}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A group of people together
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} Any company of people; an assemblage; a throng.
##* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=I|canto=IV|stanza=7|passage=There a noble '''crew''' / Of Lordes and Ladies stood on every side.}}
##* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=4|passage=Faithful to whom? to thy rebellious '''crew'''?|year=1873}}
## A group of people (often staff) manning and operating a large facility or piece of equipment such as a factory, ship, boat, airplane, or spacecraft.
##: {{ux|en|If you need help, please contact a member of the '''crew'''.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1905|author=H. G. Wells|title=The Empire of the Ants|passage=He saw now clearly that the sole '''crew''' of the vessel was these two dead men, and though he could not see their faces, he saw by their outstretched hands, which were all of ragged flesh, that they had been subjected to some strange exceptional process of decay.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023 November 29|author=Paul Clifton|title=West is best in the Highlands|journal=RAIL|issue=997|pages=37-38|text=There's a change of driver halfway at Crianlarich. Glasgow '''crews''' bring the 35-year-old Class 156 north, then wait to take over the next train back south. '''Crews''' from Mallaig, Oban and Fort William take their trains from the coast to Crianlarich and swap over. There's a tiny rest room on the platform, with a microwave and a sink, while they wait. Some drivers are signed all the way to the city. Most are not.}}
## A group of people working together on a task.
##: {{ux|en|The '''crews''' competed to cut the most timber.}}
## {{lb|en|arts}} The group of workers on a dramatic production who are not part of the [[cast]].
##: {{ux|en|There are a lot of carpenters in the '''crew'''!}}
##: {{ux|en|The '''crews''' for different movies would all come down to the bar at night.}}
## {{lb|en|informal|often|derogatory}} A close group of friends.
##: {{ux|en|I’d look out for that whole '''crew''' down at Jack’s.}}
## {{lb|en|often|derogatory}} A set of individuals lumped together by the speaker.
##* '''1861''' William Weston Patton, (version of) ''John Brown's Body''
##*: He captured Harper’s Ferry, with his nineteen men so few,
##*: And frightened "Old Virginny" till she trembled thru and thru;
##*: They hung him for a traitor, they themselves the traitor '''crew''',
##*: But his soul is marching on.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1950|author=Bernard Nicholas Schilling|title=Conservative England and the Case Against Voltaire|page=266|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=YFoOAAAAIAAJ|passage=Malignant principles bear fruit in kind and the Revolution did no more than practice what men had been taught by the abandoned '''crew''' of philosophers.}}
## {{lb|en|Scouting}} A group of [[Rovers]].
## {{lb|en|slang|hip-hop}} A [[hip-hop]] or [[b-boying]] group.
##* {{quote-song|en|year=1985|artist=w:Run-DMC|title={{w|King of Rock (song)|King of Rock}}|passage=And Jay cuts the records every day of the week / And we are the '''crew''' that can never be meek}}
##* {{quote-web|en|date=1988-02-07|author=Carly Darling|title=L.A.—The Second Deffest City of Hip-Hop|work=Los Angeles Times|url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1988-02-07-ca-41165-story.html|passage=The most popular and critically acclaimed rap and deejay “'''crews'''”—Run-D.M.C., Whodini, L.L. Cool J, the Beastie Boys, the Fat Boys, Public Enemy, Full Force, Salt & Pepa, Afrika Bambaataa, Kurtis Blow, Mantronix, U.T.F.O., et al.—were spawned on that city's streets.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=Jennifer Guglielmo; Salvatore Salerno|title=Are Italians White?|page=150|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=_yA7s3IfF6sC|isbn=0415934508|passage=We decided we needed another rapper in the '''crew''' and spent months looking.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2016|author=Sophy Smith|title=Hip-Hop Turntablism, Creativity and Collaboration|publisher=Routledge|isbn=9781317121879|page=10|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=QmgGDAAAQBAJ&pg=PA10|text=In b-boying culture, a group of b-boys or b-girls who dance and battle together are referred to as a '''crew'''.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=2021|author=Jehnie I. Burns|title=Mixtape Nostalgia: Culture, Memory, and Representation|page=138
|passage={{...}} mutating into all-star line-ups of emcees spitting hot bars over familiar beats, then to a single '''crew''' spitting bars over familiar beats, then eventually to a single crew (or artist) spitting bars over unfamiliar beats.}}
## {{lb|en|rowing}} A [[rowing]] team manning a single shell.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1888|author=[[w:Walter Bradford Woodgate|W.B. Woodgate]]|title=Boating|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=x4F6W5Twd9cC|page=71|passage=If a '''crew''' feather much under water, it is a good plan to seat them in a row on a bench, and give each man a stick to handle as an oar.}}
# A person in a crew
## {{lb|en|plural: crew}} A member of the crew of a vessel or plant.
##: {{ux|en|One '''crew''' died in the accident.}}
## {{lb|en|arts|plural: crew}} A worker on a dramatic production who is not part of the [[cast]].
##: {{ux|en|There were three actors and six '''crew''' on the set.}}
## {{lb|en|nautical|plural: crew}} A member of a ship's company who is not an [[officer]].
##: {{ux|en|The officers and '''crew''' assembled on the deck.}}
##: {{ux|en|There are quarters for three officers and five '''crew'''.}}
# {{lb|en|sports|rowing|US|uncountable}} The sport of competitive [[rowing]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1973|title=University of Virginia Undergraduate Record|passage=The University of Virginia belongs to the Atlantic Coast Conference and competes interscholastically in basketball, baseball, '''crew''', cross country, fencing, football, golf, indoor track, lacrosse, polo, soccer, swimming, tennis, track, and wrestling.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1989|author=w:Benjamin Spock; Mary Morgan|title=Spock on Spock|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=WVvWvSAMSmcC|page=71|isbn=0394578139|passage=Two Andover classmates, Al Wilson and Al Lindley, both went out for '''crew''' in our freshman year at Yale.}}

